## When you cannot sleep in the city

You know the feeling. Three days in a row above thirty degrees, scorching asphalt still radiating heat at midnight, and a flat that never quite cools down overnight. Summer heat in big cities has stopped being an amusing episode and become something people actively flee.

Mariánské Lázně is better prepared for that escape than most Czech destinations. And this is not a marketing phrase — behind it stand **geography, forest and physics**.

## Reason one: six hundred metres above sea level

Mariánské Lázně sits at an altitude that changes everything. The town centre lies at roughly **578 metres above sea level**, while the spa quarter itself rises higher still — to around **630 metres**. The official description of the local climate therefore states that, given an altitude of around 600 m, it has a **mid-mountain character**.

What does that mean in practice? Air temperature falls with altitude by roughly **0.6 to 0.65 °C per 100 metres**. Prague lies at about 200 metres. A difference of 400 vertical metres therefore accounts on its own for **approximately two and a half degrees** — and that is precisely the difference between "bearable" and "impossible to breathe".

The figures bear this out. The average annual temperature in Mariánské Lázně is **+6.4 °C**. And above all:

- **140 to 150 days a year** have a daily mean temperature above 10 °C
- **only 5 to 10 days a year** have a daily mean temperature above 20 °C

That second figure is worth pausing on — and understanding correctly.

**It does not mean that the thermometer climbs above 20 °C on only five to ten afternoons a year.** There are naturally more such days. The daily mean temperature also accounts for the night, so this indicator says something different — and, for a summer stay, something rather more useful: **the heat here does not last all day**. Even after a warm afternoon, the night cools down reliably.

And that is exactly the difference from baking cities. There the problem is not so much the afternoon peak as the fact that concrete and asphalt radiate heat well into the night, so the morning starts from an already high figure. Here the slate is wiped clean every night.

## Reason two: the town is surrounded by forest

Altitude alone would not be enough. The second factor is the **Slavkov Forest**, which encircles Mariánské Lázně on all sides.

The forest is not passive decoration — it works as **air conditioning**:

- **It shades.** Beneath the canopy, sunlight does not reach ground that would otherwise heat up and radiate that warmth back.
- **It evaporates.** Trees transpire water, and evaporation consumes energy — the forest thereby actively cools its surroundings. A mature tree can transpire hundreds of litres of water on a hot day.
- **It filters.** Coniferous forests release **phytoncides**, volatile plant compounds with antibacterial properties. The air is clean and fragrant as a result.
- **It has nowhere to store heat.** Mariánské Lázně lacks what creates the **urban heat island** in big cities — kilometres of asphalt, concrete and baking rooftops. Instead there is a sheltered valley basin, parks and trees.

There is a pleasant bonus on top: **a low allergen load**. The combination of altitude and local flora means significantly lower exposure to pollen, dust mites and mould spores than in the lowlands. Anyone who suffers in the city in summer gets relief here twice over.

## Reason three: here the climate is officially therapeutic

In **2023** Mariánské Lázně was recognised as a **climatic spa** — the sixth in the Czech Republic. This is not a title handed out lightly. It requires **regularly monitored and certified clean air** as a documented natural healing resource.

With it, Mariánské Lázně became the **only place in the world demonstrably possessing all four natural healing resources at once**:

1. **mineral waters**
2. **peat** (peloids)
3. **spring gas** (natural CO₂)
4. **healing climate**

In other words: what you experience in summer as pleasant coolness and easily breathable air is classified here as a **therapeutic resource**. We cover it in more detail on the [climate therapy](/en/climate-therapy) page.

And to be clear — it is not perpetually overcast here. The town averages **1,670 hours of sunshine a year**. The sun is there. It just comes with shade, forest and a tolerable temperature.

## How to plan a summer stay

If you are escaping the heat here, it is worth making the most of it.

### Go into the forest, not past it

A network of **more than 70 kilometres of marked trails** in three difficulty levels runs around the town. You do not need to be an athlete — the gentle routes are precisely what has always been called the **terrain cure** here: a guided walk during which the forest does the best thing it knows how to do for you. You will find tips in the [nature guide](/en/nature) or among the [day trips](/en/day-trips).

### Use the daily rhythm

Mornings and evenings here are markedly cooler than in the city. The ideal plan for a hot day looks like this:

- **morning** — the drinking cure and a walk along the [colonnade](/en/colonnade), which stays pleasant even at midday in the shade of its cast-iron arches
- **afternoon** — the forest, treatments or the pool; outdoors it is warmest between 2 and 5 p.m.
- **evening** — a concert, the [Singing Fountain](/en/magazine/singing-fountain) and a stroll for which you will need a jumper rather than a fan

### Bring something warm as well

It sounds like a joke in an article about heat, but it is practical advice: **at 600 metres the night cools quickly**. A light jacket or a jumper for the evening walk comes in handy even in July.

### Consider when to come

Summer here is lovely, but the [best time to visit](/en/best-time-to-visit) depends on what you are after. If you want to escape the heat, **July and August** are the right choice — that is exactly when the difference from the lowlands is most noticeable.

## Who benefits most from this climate

Cooler, cleaner air is not merely a question of comfort. Those who gain most are:

- **people with respiratory problems** — clean air low in allergens and particulates
- **older people and those with circulatory conditions** — for them lowland heatwaves are the most dangerous
- **allergy sufferers** — less pollen and fewer mould spores than in the city
- **families with small children** — bearable temperatures and shade instead of a scorching playground
- **anyone who badly needs a proper night's sleep** — the cool nights do most of the work

## In summary

Mariánské Lázně is not cool by accident. It is the result of three things coming together in one place: an **altitude of around 600 metres**, the **dense woods of the Slavkov Forest** and a **sheltered valley position without an urban heat island**. The result is a climate so exceptional that in 2023 the state recognised it as a **healing resource**.

While the cities bake, here you can walk, sleep and breathe. It is not an escape from summer — it is summer as it used to be.

You will find places to stay in our [hotel overview](/en/accommodation); if you are arriving by car, the [parking information](/en/parking) will be useful.
